On Mon, 10 Nov 2003, Gregory M. Nosch wrote:

> Thanks to all for comments and help.
>
> My answers are:
> SRM version-        V5.8-1
> show console-       serial

> the machine switches to serial console. If
> this is what's happening, is there a way to
> force it to send output to the vga card?

I remember posting something about this around when 3.2 came out.  The
kernel doesn't pay attention to what the SRM console variable is set to
and always defaults to serial after it boots.  (You see "rootdev on
/dev/sdwhatever" on your VGA console, and then everything else is on
serial... try setting your console to "graphics" in SRM and see it it
follows.)

I don't remember what exactly I did to work around it.  I doubt it was
hard to do.  Unfortunately the machine I did it on (AS200/233) seems to be
unreachable, or I'd go look at it and figure it out again.

> had read somewhere that the onboard IDE
> controller is very slow and that installing
> a Promise Ultra100 IDE helps improve
> drive access.

I have a PC164LX, it's onboard CMD controller is pretty lousy.  You can't
boot off the Promise though..the PC164 SRMs are rather limited in terms of
what devices they can boot from.  I ended up finding a DEC-issue
Symbios/LSI Logic SCSI card to boot from.  Maybe put a small boot drive on
the onboard IDE and run most of the system off another board and drive.

> Thank you for your honesty on this particular
> hardware. I will continue to try to get

It isn't exactly the finest DEC ever made.. because DEC didn't really make
most of them, and they were intended to be cheap(er).  I can say from
trial and error that FreeBSD (4.8?) and Linux (RH7.2) were only marginally
better than OpenBSD (3.2) on the 164LX.  Tru64 5.1 ran well enough -
probably because they've patched around the quirks in the hardware.
(Except the onboard IDE, which even HPQ thinks sucks bad enough that
they don't support it except to run the CDROM!)

I keep seeing used DS10's on eBay pretty cheap these days... wonder if
they're actually made any better.

--
